window.docContents[167].resolve({"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Linear-pushforward":{"contents":"Every continuous real-linear equivalence  induces a continuous\ncomplex-linear equivalencecharacterized byNo Jacobian factor belongs in this defining distributional pairing. A\nJacobian appears only after one chooses volume measures and rewrites the\npushforward of a regular distribution as another density.\n\nPushforward is contravariant on test functions and hence covariant on\ndistributions.\n\nLinear pushforward preserves identities and composition, and pushforward by\n is inverse to pushforward by :\n\nEach identity follows after evaluating on a Schwartz function. Identity and\ncomposition reduce to the corresponding laws for precomposition, while the\ninverse law uses .\n\nDifferentiating a pushforward requires transporting the direction back through\n: since  is differentiated along  when \nis differentiated along , the distributional statement carries .\n\nDirectional derivatives transform contravariantly under linear pushforward:\n\nEvaluate both sides on a Schwartz function and move the distributional\nderivative onto that test function. The ordinary chain rule gives\n, which is exactly the\ndisplayed identity after transposition.\n\nEquality rather than inclusion is special to invertible transport: the smooth\nproper pushforward of ordinary distributions gives only an inclusion.\n\nLinear pushforward carries distributional support to its geometric image:\n\nA pushforward vanishes on an open set  precisely when the original\ndistribution vanishes on . Since  is a homeomorphism, it maps\nthe family of open vanishing sets, and hence their union, to the corresponding\nfamily and union for . Taking complements gives the support identity.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.3. Linear pushforward","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Linear-pushforward"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Pressure-as-an-elliptic-constraint":{"contents":"Pressure has no separate evolution equation. Taking the divergence of Euler\nand using  gives\n\n\n\nThus pressure is determined nonlocally at each time, up to the boundary\nconditions and the usual additive constant. Hyperbolic transport and elliptic\npressure recovery are already coupled before viscosity enters.\n\nOne way to read this constraint is to start with the acceleration\n. Momentum asks for\n, but an incompressible evolution must also have\n. Thus : the pressure\ngradient corrects the acceleration so that it stays tangent to the\ndivergence-free constraint. Solving that elliptic problem is the nonlocal\nstep; knowing  at one point does not determine its pressure correction\nthere.\n\nOn a bounded domain with homogeneous Dirichlet data, the\nweak Poisson theorem\nof the elliptic chapter gives a variational existence-and-uniqueness model for\nthis elliptic step.\nFluid pressure more often comes with periodic, whole-space, or Neumann data\nand an additive normalization, so that theorem supplies the analytic pattern\nrather than a direct pressure solver for every fluid domain.\n\nThe pressure Poisson source and residual areThe spatial subscript distinguishes these operators from derivatives in the\ntime coordinate.\n\nIf the spatial divergence of the differentiated Euler momentum balance holds\nand , then\n\nTaking spatial divergence gives\n. The first term vanishes for a sufficiently\nregular divergence-free flow. Rearranging the remaining scalar identity gives\nthe stated Poisson equation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow\u0009Incompressible Euler Flow","header":"8.4.1.3. Pressure as an elliptic constraint","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Pressure-as-an-elliptic-constraint"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Recovering-multipliers-from-constrained-tests":{"contents":"Let  be real Hilbert spaces and let  be continuous linear\nwith closed range. If  vanishes on , then \nfactors continuously through . Moreover, there is a\nunique  such thatEquivalently,  solves the adjoint equationwhere  is the Fréchet–Riesz equivalence.This is the equation-independent Lagrange-multiplier step behind weak\npressure recovery. When  is a divergence operator with closed range,\n is the divergence-free test space and  is the pressure.\n\nFirst descend  to the Hilbert quotient . Closedness of the\nrange identifies this quotient continuously and linearly with\n. Fréchet–Riesz representation on that closed\nsubspace produces . Testing against every  gives the representation\nformula. If two range-valued multipliers represent , their difference\nis both in the range and orthogonal to the range, hence is zero. The adjoint\nidentity is the same representation formula rewritten using the defining\nproperty of .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Aubin–Lions Compactness","header":"6.3.1.10.3. Recovering multipliers from constrained tests","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Recovering-multipliers-from-constrained-tests"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Galerkin-Construction-for-Weak-Evolution--Basis-free-spectral-trial-spaces":{"contents":"For a continuous inclusion  of Hilbert spaces, its Gram operator is\n. Its spectral subspaces take the place of an eigenbasis\nchosen in advance.\n\nThe spectral construction of trial spaces needs  to be symmetric,\ncompact, and injective, and each property is inherited from .\n\nThe identitymakes the Gram operator positive and symmetric. If  is compact, then\n is compact; if  is injective, then  is injective.\n\nThe adjoint identity gives\n, which is symmetric in  and nonnegative when\n. If  is compact, composition with the bounded adjoint \nkeeps  compact. If , pairing with  gives\n; injectivity of  then forces .\n\nFor a compact symmetric operator the eigenvalues can accumulate only at\nzero, so for each threshold  only finitely many eigenvalues have\nmagnitude at least the threshold, and the sum of their eigenspaces is\nfinite-dimensional. Injectivity removes the zero eigenspace, the one part of\nthe spectral decomposition that no threshold ever reaches.\n\nFor an injective compact symmetric operator, sum all eigenspaces whose\neigenvalues have magnitude at least . Each threshold space is\nfinite-dimensional, the spaces increase with , and their union is dense\nin the energy space. Taking whole eigenspaces makes the construction\nindependent of a basis within each repeated eigenspace: the spectral subspaces\nthemselves determine the trial spaces.\n\nThe compact self-adjoint spectral theorem decomposes the orthogonal complement\nof the kernel into finite-dimensional nonzero eigenspaces, with possible\neigenvalues accumulating only at zero. Injectivity makes the kernel trivial.\nFor a fixed threshold, only finitely many eigenvalues have magnitude at least\n, so the corresponding sum is finite-dimensional. As the threshold\ndecreases, every nonzero eigenspace eventually appears; completeness of the\nspectral decomposition makes the increasing union dense.\n\nThreshold spaces are sums of eigenspaces of , hence invariant under\nit, and this invariance is what makes the energy-orthogonal projection onto\nthem pivot-orthogonal as well: for  in the threshold space,\n, and  again lies in the\nspace. A stable trial projection is therefore obtained without any choice of\nbasis.\n\nThe energy-orthogonal projection onto each Gram spectral threshold space is\nalso orthogonal after inclusion into the pivot space. It therefore defines a\nGalerkin trial projection  withThus its energy stability constant is exactly one.\n\nThe abstract construction needs only a compact Gelfand inclusion, and for\nthe parabolic problem that compactness is the Rellich theorem.\n\nOn every bounded open domain, compactness of\n specializes the abstract spectral\nconstruction to a stable trial system dense in both the energy and pivot\nspaces. This provides the finite-dimensional approximation data required by\nthe weak parabolic existence theorem.\n\nOn the interval  the two constructions agree. The sines\n are orthogonal both in  and in , so they\ndiagonalize the Gram operator, and its threshold spaces are the spans of the\nfirst  sines. The Galerkin solution in such a space is the truncated sine\nseries of the exact solution, and the error in  is the tail of the\nseries. At positive time the factor  makes that tail small very\nquickly.\n\n\n\nLet . For a continuous, uniformly bounded, and uniformly coercive\ntime-dependent form, stable finite-dimensional trial projections\ndense in both  and  produce a unique integrated weak solution for\nevery forcing in  and every initial datum in .The theorem contains both stages of Evans's Galerkin argument: solve the\nfinite-dimensional ODEs for regular forcing, then use uniform energy bounds,\nweak compactness, and density to pass to arbitrary Bochner  forcing.\n\nFirst take a bounded continuous forcing. Construct the finite-dimensional trajectories,\nextract a weak state limit using the uniform energy bound, and pass the projected\ntime-tested equations to the limit. To reach a prescribed  forcing, approximate\nit by bounded continuous loads. The  continuous-dependence estimate makes their\nsolutions Cauchy in , and strong-limit closure gives the integrated equation for\nthe prescribed load. The uniqueness theorem identifies the solution.\n\nThe unique solution just produced is a Bochner class. The residual theorem\nand Lions–Magenes attach to it a continuous pivot representative with the\nprescribed initial trace, and for continuous bounded forcing this\nrepresentative also satisfies the coercive prefix estimate.\n\nFor continuous bounded forcing, the Galerkin theorem returns the unique\nintegrated weak solution together with its continuous pivot representative,\nweak  time derivative, initial trace, and the coercive energy\nestimate on every prefix .\n\nApply the Galerkin existence theorem to obtain the unique integrated weak\nsolution. For continuous bounded forcing, the residual\n is an  weak derivative. Lions–Magenes gives\nthe continuous pivot representative and its initial trace, and the exact\nenergy identity combined with coercivity and Young's inequality gives the\nprefix estimate. Uniqueness identifies this enriched solution with the one\nalready constructed.\n\nThe measurable-coefficient theorems below remove the time continuity of \nby replacing the classical Galerkin trajectories with the Carathéodory\napproximations, and relax coercivity to a Gårding inequality by the\nexponential change of variables that turns the mass-shifted form into a\ncoercive one. Uniqueness in the Gårding case\ncomes from the integral Grönwall argument, not from nonnegativity of the\nunshifted form.\n\nLet  and let  be strongly measurable and uniformly bounded on\n. Assume the Gårding inequalityGiven stable finite-dimensional projections dense in both  and ,\nevery forcing in  and every initial datum in  determine a\nunique integrated weak solution. The full theorem additionally returns its\n weak derivative, the almost-everywhere identity\n, a continuous pivot representative with the prescribed\nleft trace, and the Gårding energy estimate on every prefix .\n\nSet . The shifted form\n is coercive, and the shifted forcing is\n. For bounded continuous loads,\nsolve the measurable finite-dimensional Carathéodory systems and pass their integrated\nequations to a weak state limit. Complete this coercive solution operator to arbitrary\n loads using the continuous-dependence estimate, and apply it to the shifted forcing.\nMultiplication by  returns a solution of the original equation.\nThe residual theorem and Lions–Magenes give the weak derivative, continuous pivot trace,\nand prefix energy estimate; Gårding uniqueness follows from the homogeneous difference\nequation and integral Gronwall.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Galerkin Construction for Weak Evolution","header":"6.3.1.8.2. Basis-free spectral trial spaces","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Galerkin-Construction-for-Weak-Evolution--Basis-free-spectral-trial-spaces"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability--Semilinear-Elliptic-Methods--Convex-Energies-in-Hilbert-Space":{"contents":"The abstract energy and resolvent results use a real Hilbert space and\nbounded bilinear forms; loads are continuous linear functionals.\n\nFor a symmetric elliptic form , nonlinear potential , and load\n, the energy is\n\nThe quadratic term supplies coercivity of : it dominates both the linear\nload and any potential bounded below by an affine function of the norm.\n\nIf  for ,  is convex and lower\nsemicontinuous, and for some  and then  is coercive and strongly convex. HenceThe hypotheses are abstract sufficient conditions.\n\nThe proof assembles three ingredients. First, coercivity: the quadratic\nterm satisfies , the potential is\nbounded below by , and the load contributes at most\n. For large norms these linear terms are absorbed, giving\n, confirming that  as\n. Second, strong convexity: for ,which follows from the quadratic identity for  and convexity of .\nThird, lower semicontinuity: the quadratic and load terms are continuous,\nwhile lower semicontinuity of  is assumed. Convexity then upgrades\nthis strong lower semicontinuity to weak lower semicontinuity through closed\nconvex sublevel sets. The coercive direct method gives existence; the strict\nconvexity inequality shows any two minimizers coincide.\n\nExistence of the minimizer used no differentiability of  at all. To\npass from a minimizer to a weak equation one needs the derivative of ,\nand this is where differentiability of the potential enters as an additional\nhypothesis, at the single point where the derivative is taken.\n\nIf  has Fréchet derivative  at , symmetry of \ngives\n\nDifferentiate the three summands of  separately. The derivative of the\nlinear load is . Bilinearity gives the derivative of\n in direction  as\n; symmetry reduces this to . Adding the\ngiven derivative of  and subtracting the load proves the formula.\n\nWith the derivative formula, the abstract first-variation theorem gives the\nweak equation. Differentiability is assumed only at the minimizer itself.\n\nEvery differentiable global minimizer satisfies the weak semilinear equationThe Lean theorem assumes the full Fréchet derivative as data.\n\nDifferentiate the energy at the minimizer, apply the vanishing first-variation\ntheorem, and evaluate the resulting zero continuous linear functional on an\narbitrary test vector.\n\nFor a differentiable convex energy , every stationary point is a global\nminimizer, so strict convexity also gives uniqueness among weak solutions.\nMonotonicity proves uniqueness directly and gives a quantitative stability\nestimate.\n\nFor a coercive bilinear form and a nonlinear dual-valued map that is monotone\nbetween two candidate states, subtracting their weak equations givesThe estimate takes the two weak equations as hypotheses and uses no\nexistence theorem or variational construction.\n\nSubtract the two weak equations evaluated at the same test vector :Choose . On the left, coercivity gives\n, and monotonicity of  gives\n. Together:If  the estimate is trivial. Otherwise divide both sides by\n and then by  to obtain the stated bound.\n\nFor the variational problem , and monotonicity is not an extra\nhypothesis: the derivative of a convex functional is monotone. Specializing\nthe abstract estimate therefore gives Lipschitz dependence on the load, and\nuniqueness with it, at no additional cost.\n\nFor a differentiable convex potential and a bilinear form with coercivity\nmodulus , weak solutions with loads  and  satisfy\n\nConvexity of  implies monotonicity of its Fréchet derivative. For any\n, convexity givesAdding these two inequalities yields\n, which is precisely monotonicity\nof the map . With this monotonicity established, the\nabstract monotone stability theorem applies directly to the two Euler–Lagrange\nequations. Setting  gives , hence\nuniqueness.\n\nExistence by minimization, the Euler equation from differentiability, and\nuniqueness from monotonicity are separate theorems with separate\nhypotheses. Under global differentiability of the convex potential they\ncombine: differentiability gives the lower semicontinuity the minimizer\ntheorem needs, the supporting hyperplane at the origin gives its affine lower\nbound, and monotonicity of  gives uniqueness through the\nload-stability estimate.\n\nFor a bounded symmetric form  with coercivity modulus , and under\nthe global differentiability and convexity assumptions on the potential,\nthe variational route produces\n\nChoose a direct-method minimizer and apply the first-variation bridge.\nConvexity makes the potential derivative monotone, so the load-stability\nestimate with equal loads proves uniqueness among all weak solutions.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Semilinear Existence and Stability\u0009Semilinear Elliptic Methods","header":"9.5.1.1. Convex Energies in Hilbert Space","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability--Semilinear-Elliptic-Methods--Convex-Energies-in-Hilbert-Space"}});